"Before printing spread, every book had to be copied by hand. A book survived only if people kept making new copies, and only if someone could read its language. When nobody copied a book for a few hundred years, it could be lost forever. So the question “who kept knowledge alive?” has many answers, in many languages.": "346ae104210a",
"Before printing spread, every book had to be copied by hand. A book survived only if people kept making new copies, and only if someone could read its language. When nobody copied a book for a few hundred years, it could be lost forever. So the question “who kept knowledge alive?” has many answers, in many languages. Some of the most famous books were written in Greek. Plato taught at the Academy in Athens, and Euclid taught geometry in Alexandria about 300 BCE. Around 150 CE Ptolemy wrote his great book of astronomy there, and the physician Galen, born in Pergamum, wrote many books on medicine. In Constantinople, Byzantine scribes went on copying Greek books for centuries. In India, scholars wrote in Sanskrit. In 628 the astronomer Brahmagupta treated zero as a number with its own rules. The numerals we use today, from 0 to 9, began in India in the 6th or 7th century. At the monastery of Nalanda, the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang learned Sanskrit; back in Chang'an from 645, he translated the scriptures he had brought into Chinese. In China, too, a Buddhist text printed in 868 is the oldest printed book that carries a date. In Baghdad, the Abbasid caliphs kept a royal library called the House of Wisdom, and their court supported many scholars. Translators turned Persian, Indian, Syriac, and Greek works into Arabic, some of them close to the court. The mathematician al-Khwārizmī wrote about Indian numerals and about algebra. A generation later, Hunayn ibn Ishaq, a Christian physician, gathered Greek manuscripts and translated Galen into Syriac and Arabic. Scholars did not only copy. Avicenna's Canon of Medicine, Ibn al-Haytham's book on optics, and al-Khwārizmī's algebra added new ideas. In Seville and Córdoba, Averroes wrote commentaries on Aristotle, which also survive in Hebrew translation. Then, in the 12th century, translators in Toledo, a city taken by the Christian king Alfonso VI in 1085, turned many of these Arabic books into Latin. Gerard of Cremona went there to learn Arabic just to read Ptolemy. In Latin Europe, monks had been copying books in monastery writing rooms all along, and the new universities after 1100 were stimulated by Arabic learning. In West Africa, Timbuktu became a center of Islamic culture and learning. Knowledge survived because people in many places, speaking Greek, Syriac, Arabic, Persian, Sanskrit, Chinese, Hebrew, and Latin, kept copying, translating, and asking new questions.": "a3a016a1489b",
